<section class="nc-guide" id="guide">
  <div class="prose">
    <h2>A worked example: the default 16 × 12 room</h2>
    <p>With the calculator's starting numbers (a 16 × 12 ft room, 10% waste, boxes covering 20.4 sq ft at $3.49 per sq ft), the order works out like this:</p>
  </div>
  <div class="table-wrap"><table>
    <thead><tr><th>Step</th><th class="num">Result</th></tr></thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr><td>Floor area, 16 × 12 ft</td><td class="num">192 sq ft</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Add 10% waste</td><td class="num">211.2 sq ft</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Divide by 20.4 sq ft per box</td><td class="num">10.35 boxes</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Round up to whole boxes</td><td class="num">11 boxes (224.4 sq ft)</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Material cost at $3.49 per sq ft</td><td class="num">$783.16</td></tr>
    </tbody>
  </table></div>
  <div class="prose">
    <p>Rounding up to whole boxes adds another 13.2 sq ft on top of the waste allowance, and $46 to the bill compared with buying exactly 211.2 sq ft. That is normal, and the spare planks are worth keeping: a damaged board years from now is much easier to replace from your own box than to match from a new production run.</p>

    <h2>How much waste to allow</h2>
  </div>
  <div class="table-wrap"><table>
    <thead><tr><th>Layout or product</th><th class="num">Typical waste</th></tr></thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr><td>Vinyl plank or laminate, straight layout, simple room</td><td class="num">5–10%</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Solid or engineered hardwood, straight layout</td><td class="num">8–10%</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Rooms with many corners, closets, or doorways</td><td class="num">10–12%</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Diagonal layout</td><td class="num">15%</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Herringbone or chevron</td><td class="num">15–20%</td></tr>
    </tbody>
  </table></div>
  <div class="prose">
    <p>Raise the allowance for long or wide planks, products with strong grain patterns you want to balance, and first-time installers. Carpet is a different case: it comes off rolls of fixed width (12 ft is the most common), so waste depends on where the seams fall rather than on a percentage. Get carpet measured from a seam diagram.</p>

    <h2>What the flooring price doesn't include</h2>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Underlayment and vapor barrier,</strong> unless the planks have it attached. Price it by the same square footage.</li>
      <li><strong>Transitions</strong> at every doorway where the floor meets a different surface.</li>
      <li><strong>Trim.</strong> Removing baseboards or adding quarter-round means buying molding by the linear foot. The 16 × 12 room has a 56 ft perimeter, less the width of its doorways.</li>
      <li><strong>Floor preparation.</strong> Leveling compound for dips, and removing the old floor if it can't stay.</li>
    </ul>

    <h2>Before you order</h2>
    <ol>
      <li>Measure each room at its widest points, and include closets as separate rectangles.</li>
      <li>Count stairs separately: they are priced per step with stair nose pieces, not by area.</li>
      <li>Buy every box at once and check that the lot numbers match.</li>
      <li>Follow the manufacturer's acclimation instructions. Many wood and laminate products must sit in the room for a set time before installation, and skipping it can void the warranty.</li>
    </ol>
  </div>
  <section class="hub-faq" aria-labelledby="guide-faq-title"><h2 id="guide-faq-title">Flooring questions</h2>
    <details><summary>How much flooring do I need for a 12 × 12 room?</summary><p>A 12 × 12 room is 144 sq ft. With 10% waste, order about 158.4 sq ft. With boxes that cover 20.4 sq ft, that is 8 boxes.</p></details>
    <details><summary>How much extra flooring should I buy?</summary><p>Plan on 5 to 10% extra for a straight layout in a simple room, about 15% for diagonal layouts, and 15 to 20% for herringbone. Round up to whole boxes after adding waste.</p></details>
    <details><summary>Should I keep leftover flooring?</summary><p>Yes. Keep at least a few planks from the same lot for repairs. Matching color and finish from a later production run can be difficult.</p></details>
  </section>
